  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes the structure of lipoprotein(a), showing it as an LDL-like particle with an added apolipoprotein(a) chain. The main spherical particle has a lipid core containing cholesteryl esters and triglycerides, surrounded by a phospholipid and free cholesterol surface. ApoB100 wraps around this LDL-like particle, while apo(a) is attached to it through a disulfide bond.\n\nApo(a) is depicted as a string of repeated kringle domains labeled KIV, followed by KV and a protease-like domain. One key point is that the KIV2 region is repeated a variable number of times, explaining apo(a) isoform size heterogeneity. The surface also includes oxidized phospholipids, suggesting that these lipids associate with lipoprotein(a).\n\nThe lower schematic compares apo(a) with plasminogen, emphasizing their structural similarity. Both contain kringle motifs and a protease domain, but apo(a) differs in function: its lysine-binding sites vary in strength, with weaker binding in some kringles and a stronger site near KIV10, and its protease domain is shown as inactive. Overall, the figure highlights how lipoprotein(a) combines an LDL particle with a plasminogen-like apo(a), helping explain its distinctive biological properties.",
